      "id": "47e3d968-68a5-48fe-8483-e4fe224360e8",
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "Great example to learn **generics, wildcards, and type erasure** 👍\nI’ll walk you through this code **line by line**, explain **why things are written this way**, and point out **what Java allows vs forbids**.\n\n---\n\n## 1️⃣ Class hierarchy (foundation)\n\n### `Student` (base class)\n\n```java\npublic class Student {\n    private String name;\n    private String course;\n    private int yearStarted;\n\n    protected static Random random = new Random();\n```\n\n* Generates **random student data**\n* `protected static Random random`\n  → accessible to subclasses like `LPAStudent`\n\n```java\n@Override\npublic String toString() {\n    return \"%-15s %-15s %d\".formatted(name, course, yearStarted);\n}\n```\n\n➡️ Every student prints neatly in columns\n\n---\n\n### `LPAStudent` (subclass)\n\n```java\npublic class LPAStudent extends Student {\n    private double percentComplete;\n```\n\n* Inherits everything from `Student`\n* Adds **extra data** (`percentComplete`)\n\n```java\n@Override\npublic String toString() {\n    return \"%s %8.1f%%\".formatted(super.toString(), percentComplete);\n}\n```\n\n➡️ Calls parent `toString()` + appends percentage\n✔️ Classic **polymorphism**\n\n---\n\n## 2️⃣ Why `List<Student>` can hold `LPAStudent`\n\n```java\nList<Student> students = new ArrayList<>();\nstudents.add(new Student());\nstudents.add(new LPAStudent());\n```\n\n✔️ **Allowed** because:\n\n> `LPAStudent IS-A Student`\n\n⚠️ But this does **NOT** work the other way:\n\n```java\nList<LPAStudent> lpaStudents = new ArrayList<>();\n// lpaStudents.add(new Student()); ❌ compile-time error\n```\n\n---\n\n## 3️⃣ `printMoreLists(List<? extends Student>)`\n\n```java\npublic static void printMoreLists(List<? extends Student> students)\n```\n\n### 🔑 What does `? extends Student` mean?\n\n➡️ **Any list whose elements are `Student` or subclasses of `Student`**\n\nThis method can accept:\n\n* `List<Student>`\n* `List<LPAStudent>`\n\nThat’s why **both calls work**:\n\n```java\nprintMoreLists(students);\nprintMoreLists(lpaStudents);\n```\n\n---\n\n### Inside the method\n\n```java\nfor (var student : students) {\n    System.out.println(student);\n}\n```\n\n* Java knows **every element is at least a `Student`**\n* Safe to read and print\n\n🚫 But you **cannot add** to this list:\n\n```java\nstudents.add(new Student()); // ❌ NOT allowed\n```\n\n#### WHY?\n\nBecause Java doesn’t know the *exact* type:\n\n* Could be `List<LPAStudent>`\n* Adding `Student` would break type safety\n\n📌 **Rule**\n\n> `? extends T` → **READ ONLY**\n\n---\n\n## 4️⃣ `testList(List<?>)` – Unbounded wildcard\n\n```java\npublic static void testList(List<?> list)\n```\n\n### Meaning\n\n➡️ Accepts **ANY List**\n\n* `List<String>`\n* `List<Integer>`\n* `List<Student>`\n* etc.\n\nThat’s why these work:\n\n```java\ntestList(new ArrayList<String>(List.of(\"Able\", \"Barry\", \"Charlie\")));\ntestList(new ArrayList<Integer>(List.of(1, 2, 3)));\n```\n\n---\n\n### Pattern matching with `instanceof`\n\n```java\nfor (var element : list) {\n    if (element instanceof String s) {\n        System.out.println(\"String: \" + s.toUpperCase());\n    } else if (element instanceof Integer i) {\n        System.out.println(\"Integer: \" + i.floatValue());\n    }\n}\n```\n\n* `element` type is **Object**\n* You **must check** before using type-specific methods\n\n✔️ Java 16+ pattern matching\nNo manual casting needed 👍\n\n---\n\n### Why not overload `testList`?\n\nYou commented this out 👇\n\n```java\npublic static void testList(List<String> list) { }\npublic static void testList(List<Integer> list) { }\n```\n\n❌ **Compile-time error**\n\n### WHY? → **Type Erasure**\n\nAt runtime:\n\n```java\nList<String> → List\nList<Integer> → List\n```\n\nBoth methods become:\n\n```java\ntestList(List list)\n```\n\n➡️ Same signature → conflict\n\n📌 **This is why `List<?>` exists**\n\n---\n\n## 5️⃣ Why this method also works\n\n```java\n// public static <T extends Student> void printList(List<T> students)\n```\n\n### Generic method version\n\n```java\n<T extends Student>\n```\n\nMeans:\n\n* `T` must be `Student` or subclass\n* Functionally similar to `? extends Student`\n\n### Difference\n\n| Feature             | `? extends Student` | `<T extends Student>` |\n| ------------------- | ------------------- | --------------------- |\n| Type name available | ❌                   | ✅                     |\n| Can return `T`      | ❌                   | ✅                     |\n| Common for reading  | ✅                   | ✅                     |\n\n---\n\n## 6️⃣ Key rules to remember (exam + interviews)\n\n### 🔹 Invariance\n\n```java\nList<LPAStudent> ≠ List<Student>\n```\n\n---\n\n### 🔹 Wildcard rules (VERY IMPORTANT)\n\n| Syntax              | Can read   | Can add |\n| ------------------- | ---------- | ------- |\n| `List<T>`           | ✅          | ✅       |\n| `List<? extends T>` | ✅          | ❌       |\n| `List<?>`           | ✅ (Object) | ❌       |\n| `List<? super T>`   | ❌          | ✅       |\n\n📌 Mnemonic:\n\n> **PECS**\n> Producer → `extends`\n> Consumer → `super`\n\n---\n\n## 7️⃣ Why this code is written this way\n\nThis lesson demonstrates:\n✔️ Polymorphism\n✔️ Wildcards\n✔️ Type erasure\n✔️ Why Java forbids certain overloads\n✔️ Safe generic design\n\n---\n\n## 8️⃣ Mental model (important)\n\nThink of wildcards as **“promises”**\n\n* `? extends Student`\n  → “I promise everything inside is a Student or better”\n* `?`\n  → “I know nothing except it’s an Object”\n\n---\n\nIf you want, next we can:\n\n* 🔍 Convert this to **real-world analogy**\n* 🧪 Rewrite with **`super` wildcard**\n* ❓ Do **interview-style trick questions**\n* 🧠 Draw a **memory diagram** of generics\n\nJust tell me 👍\n",
